WEBVTT

00:00:05.508 --> 00:00:07.886
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> Hello, and welcome to <i>Science Now</i>.

00:00:09.177 --> 00:00:12.247
When we think of famous inventors, we often think of men –

00:00:12.684 --> 00:00:17.801
like Alexander Graham Bell, Guglielmo Marconi, and Thomas Edison.

00:00:18.906 --> 00:00:24.206
But many of the things which make our lives easier or safer today were invented by women.

00:00:25.357 --> 00:00:27.783
And on today’s show, we’re going to look at six of them.

00:00:31.962 --> 00:00:37.103
One invention that definitely improved the lives of millions of people was the disposable nappy.

00:00:37.832 --> 00:00:40.875
They were invented by a woman called Marion Donovan.

00:00:42.359 --> 00:00:47.240
Her father and uncle were inventors and, when she had young children,

00:00:47.716 --> 00:00:51.761
she invented a nappy that you could use and then throw away.

00:00:56.121 --> 00:01:00.749
Before her invention, babies wore nappies made of cotton – like these.

00:01:01.959 --> 00:01:04.032
And this meant a lot of washing.

00:01:09.943 --> 00:01:13.482
Although she invented the disposable nappy in the 1940s,

00:01:13.874 --> 00:01:19.164
it wasn’t until 1951 that an American company bought Donovan’s idea.

00:01:20.717 --> 00:01:24.137
Today, millions of disposable nappies are used every day,

00:01:24.559 --> 00:01:27.371
and Donovan’s invention is now more eco-friendly.

00:01:29.906 --> 00:01:32.034
You can buy biodegradable nappies!

00:01:36.892 --> 00:01:40.141
And now another invention which has made life easier.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.416
The dishwasher was invented by a woman called Josephine Cochrane in 1886.

00:01:48.234 --> 00:01:51.357
She was a rich American who gave a lot of dinner parties.

00:01:51.882 --> 00:01:55.478
But she was annoyed that her servants often broke plates and glasses

00:01:55.523 --> 00:01:57.214
when they were washing up after a party.

00:02:00.898 --> 00:02:01.578
Oops!

00:02:03.832 --> 00:02:09.512
So, Cochrane decided to invent a machine which could wash a lot of plates and glasses safely.

00:02:10.687 --> 00:02:15.970
She said, ‘If nobody else is going to invent a dishwasher, then I will!’

00:02:18.876 --> 00:02:22.614
She designed the machine, and then she found a company to make it.

00:02:27.423 --> 00:02:31.344
At first, only hotels and restaurants bought Cochrane’s new machine,

00:02:31.979 --> 00:02:35.873
but today, the dishwasher is used in homes all over the world.

00:02:44.107 --> 00:02:47.122
The car was invented by a man, but it was a woman,

00:02:47.123 --> 00:02:51.004
Mary Anderson, who solved one of the biggest problems of driving.

00:02:53.547 --> 00:02:59.473
On a trip to New York in 1903, Anderson noticed that drivers had to open their windows

00:02:59.474 --> 00:03:02.145
to see where they were going when it was raining.

00:03:06.379 --> 00:03:12.334
When she returned to her home in Alabama, she invented windscreen wipers,

00:03:12.666 --> 00:03:14.987
which made driving a lot safer.

00:03:20.923 --> 00:03:25.499
Our fourth invention made life safer for people living in flats or apartments –

00:03:26.114 --> 00:03:28.041
video entry phones.

00:03:30.595 --> 00:03:37.864
They were invented in 1966 by Marie Van Brittan Brown, a nurse who lived in New York.

00:03:41.233 --> 00:03:45.486
Her neighbourhood was quite dangerous and Brown was often at home alone,

00:03:46.101 --> 00:03:50.010
so she decided to invent something that would make her feel safer.

00:03:51.813 --> 00:03:55.932
With the help of her husband, an electrician, she developed a camera

00:03:55.933 --> 00:03:57.597
that you could put by the front door.

00:03:58.466 --> 00:04:02.979
This camera sent a picture of the person at the door to a screen inside the flat,

00:04:03.714 --> 00:04:07.661
and there was also a microphone so you could talk to the person outside.

00:04:09.993 --> 00:04:13.693
If the person was a welcome visitor, you pushed a button to let them in.

00:04:14.464 --> 00:04:18.311
If not, you could push another button to contact a security company.

00:04:19.536 --> 00:04:22.624
This system is now common all over the world.

00:04:35.967 --> 00:04:38.923
And now an invention that has saved a lot of lives.

00:04:39.916 --> 00:04:46.997
The modern life raft was invented by Maria Beasley in 1880 and then improved in 1882.

00:04:48.881 --> 00:04:52.478
Before her invention, life rafts were just wooden platforms,

00:04:52.766 --> 00:04:55.541
and many people died in accidents at sea.

00:04:57.793 --> 00:05:03.091
Four of her life rafts were used on the <i>Titanic</i>, alongside the ship’s normal lifeboats.

00:05:04.299 --> 00:05:10.023
When the <i>Titanic</i> sank in 1912, only 706 people survived,

00:05:10.613 --> 00:05:14.675
and nearly 200 of them were in the life rafts designed by Beasley.

00:05:24.197 --> 00:05:28.700
Our last invention is one of today’s most important green technologies.

00:05:31.927 --> 00:05:35.821
Mária Telkes, a Hungarian-American scientist, worked at the

00:05:35.822 --> 00:05:40.351
Massachusetts Institute of Technology in the United States in the 1940s.

00:05:41.251 --> 00:05:46.412
She was so interested in the power of the sun, that her nickname was ‘the Sun Queen’.

00:05:48.756 --> 00:05:49.886
In nineteen forty-eight,

00:05:49.887 --> 00:05:53.329
she designed the first house which was heated completely by the sun.

00:05:54.745 --> 00:05:56.709
The system worked for three years.

00:05:59.609 --> 00:06:04.036
The solar technology we use today is very different from Telkes’s invention,

00:06:04.636 --> 00:06:10.370
but – like all our female inventors – she showed the world what was possible.

00:06:11.318 --> 00:06:17.268
And today solar technology has the power to change our lives and save our environment.